# In Silico-Based Novel Antigen Identification in *Rhipicephalus microplus*

[License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)
[Workflow](https://img.shields.io/badge/Workflow-Subtractive%20Proteomics-green.svg)]()
[Organism](https://img.shields.io/badge/Organism-*R.%20microplus*-orange.svg)]()

# Overview

This repository contains a reproducible bioinformatics workflow for
the identification and prioritization of novel antigen candidates
from the *Rhipicephalus microplus* proteome using an integrated
subtractive proteomics approach.

The workflow combines sequence redundancy reduction, host-homology
filtering, essentiality analysis, subcellular localization,
antigenicity prediction, allergenicity and toxicity screening,
sequence conservation, functional annotation, and candidate
prioritization.

The primary objective is to identify *R. microplus* proteins that
may represent promising antigen candidates for subsequent
experimental investigation and vaccine-development studies.

## Biological Objective

Ticks represent important vectors and ectoparasites affecting
livestock health. Identification of suitable antigenic targets can
support the development of alternative approaches for tick control.

The objective of this computational study is to systematically
screen the *R. microplus* proteome and prioritize proteins based on
multiple biological and immunological characteristics.

The analysis focuses on proteins that satisfy a combination of
criteria including:

- Essential biological function
- Lack of significant similarity to selected host proteins
- Predicted antigenicity
- Favorable subcellular localization
- Predicted non-allergenicity
- Predicted non-toxicity
- Sequence conservation
- Functional relevance


## Study Organism

Organism: Rhipicephalus microplus

Reference assembly: GCF_013339725.1

Data type: Protein sequences

Primary database: NCBI

The exact accession, release information, and download date are
documented 

## Host Organisms

Host-homology subtraction is performed against the selected host
proteomes.

The current analysis considers:

. Bos taurus
. Homo sapiens

The purpose of this filtering step is to reduce the likelihood of
prioritizing proteins with substantial similarity to host proteins.

# Computational Workflow

                    . R. microplus Proteome
                              
                    . Proteome Quality Control
                              
                    . Redundancy Reduction
                             
                    . Host-Homology Subtraction
                              
                     . Essentiality Analysis
                             
                  . Subcellular Localization
                             
                     . Antigenicity Prediction
                              
                . Allergenicity / Toxicity Screening
                              
                     . Conservation Analysis
                              
                     . Functional Annotation
                             
                    . Candidate Prioritization
                             
                   . Novel Antigen Candidates
